// Java Backend – questions contributed by the project owner (java_backend_engineer_300_interview_questions.csv),
// part 1: core Java, OOP, strings, collections, Java 8 and exceptions. Ids jb-NNN keep the CSV number.
// Answers, key points and follow-ups were written for V1.4.
import { kp, Q } from './kp.js';

const C = 'Core Java & OOP', S = 'Core Java & OOP', CO = 'Collections', J = 'JVM & Performance', M = 'Java 8+ Features', E = 'Exceptions', T = 'Troubleshooting';
const src = (n) => ({ source: `owner-csv-${n}` });

export const JB_CORE = [
  Q('jb-001', C, 'EM', 'concept', 'What are the main features of Java, and why is Java platform-independent?',
    [kp('Compiled to bytecode, run by the JVM', 'bytecode', 'jvm'), kp('Write once, run anywhere: a JVM per platform', 'write once', 'run anywhere', 'each platform', 'every platform'), kp('Object-oriented, strongly typed', 'object oriented', 'strongly typed', 'statically typed'), kp('Automatic memory management / garbage collection', 'garbage collect*', 'memory management'), kp('Multithreading, security and a rich standard library', 'multithread*', 'standard library', 'secur*')],
    'Java is an object oriented, strongly typed language with automatic memory management through garbage collection, built in multithreading, a strong security model and a rich standard library. It is platform independent because javac does not compile to machine code: it compiles source into bytecode in class files. That bytecode runs on a Java Virtual Machine, and there is a JVM for each platform, such as Windows, Linux or macOS, which interprets and JIT compiles the bytecode to native code. So the same jar can be written once and run anywhere a compatible JVM exists. The JVM itself is platform specific; the bytecode is not.',
    'Is the JVM itself platform-independent?', src(1)),

  Q('jb-003', J, 'EM', 'concept', 'How does Java code get compiled and executed? Explain the complete lifecycle.',
    [kp('javac compiles .java to .class bytecode', 'javac', 'bytecode', 'class file*'), kp('Class loader loads, links and initialises classes', 'class loader', 'classloader', 'load*'), kp('Bytecode verification', 'verif*'), kp('Interpreter then JIT compiles hot code to native', 'interpret*', 'jit', 'just in time', 'native code'), kp('Runtime memory areas and garbage collection', 'heap', 'stack', 'garbage collect*')],
    'First javac compiles the .java source into platform neutral bytecode stored in .class files, usually packaged in a jar. When you run the program, the JVM starts and the class loader subsystem loads classes on demand, then links them: the bytecode verifier checks the code is safe, static fields are prepared and symbolic references are resolved, and finally static initialisers run. The execution engine starts by interpreting bytecode, profiles which methods are hot, and the JIT compiler (C1 and C2) compiles those into optimised native code. Objects live on the heap, each thread has its own stack of frames, and the garbage collector reclaims unreachable objects until the JVM exits.',
    'What is the difference between the C1 and C2 JIT compilers?', src(3)),

  Q('jb-004', C, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the difference between primitive and reference data types?',
    [kp('Eight primitives store the value directly', 'primitive*', 'int', 'boolean', 'value directly'), kp('Reference types store a reference to an object on the heap', 'reference*', 'heap', 'object*'), kp('Defaults: 0/false vs null', 'null', 'default'), kp('Wrapper classes and autoboxing', 'wrapper*', 'autobox*', 'integer')],
    'Java has eight primitive types: byte, short, int, long, float, double, char and boolean. A primitive variable holds the value directly, has a fixed size, cannot be null and defaults to zero or false as a field. Reference types, such as classes, interfaces, arrays and String, hold a reference that points to an object on the heap; the default is null, and comparing with == compares references, not contents. Primitives are faster and use less memory. Collections and generics need reference types, so each primitive has a wrapper class such as Integer, and autoboxing converts between them, which can cost allocation and throw NullPointerException when unboxing null.',
    'What problems can autoboxing cause in a tight loop?', src(4)),

  Q('jb-006', C, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the difference between abstraction and encapsulation?',
    [kp('Abstraction: expose what an object does, hide complexity', 'abstraction', 'what it does', 'hide complexity', 'hides complexity'), kp('Achieved with interfaces and abstract classes', 'interface*', 'abstract class*'), kp('Encapsulation: bundle data with methods, restrict access', 'encapsulation', 'private', 'access modifier*'), kp('Protects invariants via getters/setters or behaviour methods', 'invariant*', 'getter*', 'setter*', 'validat*')],
    'Abstraction is a design level idea: show what an object does and hide how it does it, so callers depend on a simple contract. In Java we achieve it with interfaces and abstract classes; a PaymentGateway interface with a charge method hides whether it calls Stripe or a bank. Encapsulation is an implementation level idea: bundle the data and the methods that operate on it in one class and restrict direct access with private fields and access modifiers. Callers go through methods, often getters, setters or better behaviour methods, which validate input and protect invariants. Abstraction hides complexity; encapsulation hides internal state.',
    'Is a class with public getters and setters for every field really encapsulated?', src(6)),

  Q('jb-008', C, 'EM', 'concept', 'Can we override static, private, and final methods in Java? Why?',
    [kp('Static methods are hidden, not overridden', 'static', 'method hiding', 'hidden', 'hiding'), kp('Resolved at compile time by reference type', 'compile time', 'reference type', 'static binding'), kp('Private methods are not inherited / not visible', 'private', 'not inherited', 'not visible'), kp('Final methods cannot be overridden (compile error)', 'final', 'compile error', 'compilation error')],
    'No in all three cases. Static methods belong to the class, not the instance, so a subclass method with the same signature only hides the parent method; the call is resolved at compile time from the reference type, which is static binding, not runtime polymorphism. Private methods are not inherited and are not visible to the subclass, so a method with the same name in the subclass is a completely new method. Final methods are explicitly locked against overriding; trying to override one is a compile error. Only non static, non private, non final instance methods participate in dynamic dispatch.',
    'What happens if you call a hidden static method through a subclass instance?', src(8)),

  Q('jb-011', C, 'MS', 'concept', 'Explain composition versus inheritance. Which one should generally be preferred?',
    [kp('Inheritance is an is-a relationship', 'inheritance', 'is a', 'extends'), kp('Composition is a has-a relationship via fields', 'composition', 'has a', 'delegat*'), kp('Inheritance couples tightly, fragile base class', 'tight* coupl*', 'fragile base class', 'coupling'), kp('Prefer composition for flexibility and testability', 'prefer composition', 'favour composition', 'favor composition', 'flexib*')],
    'Inheritance models an is a relationship: a subclass extends a parent and reuses its code, but it is tightly coupled to the parent implementation, which leads to the fragile base class problem, deep hierarchies and broken encapsulation. Composition models a has a relationship: a class holds other objects as fields and delegates work to them, often through interfaces. That is more flexible, because behaviour can be swapped at runtime or injected, and it is easier to test with mocks. The usual guidance is to prefer composition over inheritance, and use inheritance only for a genuine is a relationship with a stable base, for example a template method in a framework.',
    'How does the Strategy pattern use composition?', src(11)),

  Q('jb-012', C, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is runtime polymorphism? Explain with an example.',
    [kp('Method overriding in a subclass', 'overrid*'), kp('Parent reference to child object', 'parent reference', 'reference of', 'superclass reference', 'interface reference'), kp('Resolved at runtime: dynamic method dispatch', 'runtime', 'dynamic dispatch', 'dynamic method dispatch', 'late binding'), kp('Concrete example', 'shape', 'payment', 'animal', 'example')],
    'Runtime polymorphism means the method that runs is decided at runtime from the actual object type, not the reference type. It comes from method overriding and is called dynamic method dispatch or late binding. For example, Payment has a pay method and CardPayment and UpiPayment override it. With Payment p = new UpiPayment(); p.pay() calls the UpiPayment version even though the reference is the parent type. The JVM looks it up through the virtual method table. This lets code depend on an interface or superclass reference and work with any implementation, which is the basis of patterns like Strategy and of Spring injecting interfaces.',
    'Is method overloading an example of runtime polymorphism?', src(12)),

  Q('jb-013', C, 'MS', 'concept', 'Explain the use of static, final, this, and super keywords.',
    [kp('static: belongs to the class, shared across instances', 'static', 'class level', 'shared'), kp('final: cannot be reassigned, overridden or extended', 'final', 'reassign*', 'constant'), kp('this: current object, constructor chaining', 'this', 'current object', 'current instance'), kp('super: parent members and constructor', 'super', 'parent constructor', 'parent class')],
    'static marks a member that belongs to the class rather than an instance: static fields are shared by all objects and static methods can be called without an instance, which suits constants and utility methods. final means it cannot change: a final variable cannot be reassigned, so final static fields are constants, a final method cannot be overridden and a final class cannot be extended. this refers to the current object; it disambiguates fields from parameters and this() chains to another constructor of the same class. super refers to the parent class: super.method() calls the parent version and super() calls the parent constructor, which must be the first statement.',
    'Can you use this or super inside a static method?', src(13)),

  Q('jb-014', C, 'MS', 'concept', 'What happens when a variable, method, or class is declared final?',
    [kp('Final variable: assigned once, cannot be reassigned', 'final variable', 'reassign*', 'assigned once'), kp('Final reference: object can still be mutated', 'mutat*', 'object can still', 'contents'), kp('Final method: cannot be overridden', 'final method', 'overrid*'), kp('Final class: cannot be subclassed, e.g. String', 'final class', 'subclass*', 'extended', 'string'), kp('Safe publication / effectively final for lambdas', 'lambda*', 'effectively final', 'safe publication', 'immutab*')],
    'A final variable can be assigned only once, either at declaration or in the constructor for fields; after that it cannot be reassigned. For a reference this only fixes the reference: a final List can still be mutated, so final is not the same as immutable. A final method cannot be overridden by subclasses, which protects critical behaviour. A final class cannot be extended, which is why String and the wrapper classes are final. Final fields also get safe publication guarantees under the Java Memory Model, which helps immutable objects, and lambdas and anonymous classes can only capture local variables that are final or effectively final.',
    'Why are lambdas restricted to effectively final local variables?', src(14)),

  Q('jb-015', C, 'MS', 'concept', 'Explain equals(), hashCode(), and toString().',
    [kp('equals: logical equality, default is reference equality', 'equals', 'logical equality', 'reference equality'), kp('hashCode: bucket index for hash-based collections', 'hashcode', 'hashmap', 'bucket*', 'hashset'), kp('Contract: equal objects must have equal hash codes', 'contract', 'equal hash', 'same hash'), kp('toString: readable representation for logs', 'tostring', 'readable', 'log*'), kp('Override together, use the same fields', 'override* together', 'same fields', 'objects.hash', 'record*')],
    'All three come from Object. equals defines logical equality; the default implementation is reference equality, so value objects override it to compare fields. hashCode returns an int that hash based collections such as HashMap and HashSet use to choose a bucket. The contract says that if two objects are equal by equals they must return the same hash code, while unequal objects may collide. So you override them together and use the same fields, for example with Objects.equals and Objects.hash, or let a record generate them. Otherwise a HashSet can keep duplicates or a HashMap lookup fails. toString returns a readable representation, which matters for logs and debugging, but it should never print secrets.',
    'Why should fields used in hashCode not change while the object is in a HashSet?', src(15)),

  Q('jb-018', J, 'MS', 'concept', 'Are Java objects always created on the heap? Explain escape analysis.',
    [kp('Logically on the heap per the language model', 'heap', 'logically'), kp('JIT escape analysis checks if object leaves method/thread', 'escape analysis', 'escape*', 'jit'), kp('Scalar replacement / stack allocation', 'scalar replacement', 'stack allocation', 'allocated on the stack', 'registers'), kp('Lock elision for non-escaping objects', 'lock elision', 'elide', 'synchroniz*'), kp('Reduces GC pressure, not guaranteed', 'gc pressure', 'garbage', 'not guaranteed')],
    'From the language point of view every object is created on the heap. In practice the HotSpot JIT compiler runs escape analysis on hot code: it checks whether an object escapes the method or thread, for example by being returned, stored in a field or passed somewhere unknown. If it does not escape, the JIT can apply scalar replacement, breaking the object into its fields kept in registers or on the stack, so no heap allocation happens at all. It can also do lock elision and remove synchronized blocks on objects only one thread can see. This reduces allocation and GC pressure, but it is an optimisation, only after warm up, and not guaranteed.',
    'Which coding patterns stop escape analysis from working?', src(18)),

  Q('jb-023', S, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the Java String Constant Pool?',
    [kp('Special area of the heap for string literals', 'string pool', 'constant pool', 'heap'), kp('Literals are interned and reused', 'literal*', 'reuse*', 'intern*'), kp('new String creates a separate object', 'new string', 'separate object', 'new object'), kp('Saves memory, safe because String is immutable', 'saves memory', 'memory', 'immutab*')],
    'The String constant pool, or string pool, is a special table inside the heap since Java 7 where the JVM keeps one shared copy of each string literal. When the code contains String a = "Java" and String b = "Java", both variables point to the same pooled object, so literals are reused instead of duplicated, which saves memory. Compile time constant expressions are pooled too. Using new String("Java") always creates a separate object on the heap, outside the pool, unless you call intern on it. Sharing is only safe because String is immutable; if strings could change, one caller would corrupt every other user of the literal.',
    'Can strings in the pool be garbage collected?', src(23)),

  Q('jb-025', S, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the output of String s1 = "Java"; String s2 = new String("Java"); System.out.println(s1 == s2)?',
    [kp('Prints false', 'false'), kp('s1 refers to the pooled literal', 'pool*', 'literal'), kp('new String creates a distinct heap object', 'new string', 'distinct', 'separate object', 'new object'), kp('== compares references; equals compares content', 'compares references', 'reference*', 'equals')],
    'It prints false. s1 points to the literal "Java" in the string pool. new String("Java") always creates a new, distinct object on the heap whose content is copied from the literal, so s2 refers to a separate object. The == operator compares references, not content, and the two references are different. s1.equals(s2) would print true because equals compares the characters. If you wrote s2 = new String("Java").intern(), or compared two literals, == would print true because both would point to the pooled instance. The lesson for production code is to always use equals for string comparison.',
    'What does s1 == s2.intern() print, and why?', src(25)),

  Q('jb-026', S, 'MS', 'concept', 'What does the intern() method do?',
    [kp('Returns the canonical pooled instance', 'canonical', 'pool*', 'intern'), kp('Adds the string to the pool if absent', 'adds', 'if absent', 'not already'), kp('Allows reference comparison of interned strings', 'reference*', 'share one object'), kp('Deduplicates many repeated strings; cost and alternatives', 'deduplicat*', 'repeated', 'g1 string dedup*', 'cost')],
    'intern returns the canonical representation of a string from the string pool. If the pool already contains an equal string, intern returns that pooled instance; if not, it adds this string to the pool and returns it. After interning, equal strings share one object, so == works as a reference comparison and memory is saved when the same values repeat millions of times, for example country codes parsed from a file. It has a cost, because the pool is a hash table and lookups are not free, so use it selectively. For general duplicate strings the JVM option G1 string deduplication can do this automatically without code changes.',
    'When could interning strings actually hurt performance?', src(26)),

  Q('jb-027', S, 'MS', 'concept', 'Why is StringBuilder generally preferred over String concatenation in loops?',
    [kp('String is immutable: each + creates a new object', 'immutab*', 'new string', 'new object'), kp('Copying makes the loop O(n^2)', 'quadratic', 'n squared', 'copy*', 'copies'), kp('StringBuilder appends into a resizable buffer', 'stringbuilder', 'append*', 'buffer'), kp('Less garbage and GC pressure', 'garbage', 'gc', 'allocation*')],
    'String is immutable, so s = s + x inside a loop creates a new String object each iteration and copies all the characters built so far. Over n iterations that copying is quadratic, roughly n squared work, and it creates a lot of short lived garbage that increases GC pressure. StringBuilder keeps a mutable, resizable character buffer, and append adds to it in amortised constant time, growing the buffer only occasionally. You call toString once at the end. The compiler optimises a single expression like a + b + c, but it cannot remove the repeated allocation across loop iterations, so builders matter in loops. Presizing with an initial capacity helps further.',
    'Why is StringBuilder preferred over StringBuffer in most code?', src(27)),

  Q('jb-031', S, 'MS', 'concept', 'How does Java handle string concatenation internally?',
    [kp('Before Java 9: compiled to StringBuilder appends', 'stringbuilder', 'append*', 'java 8'), kp('Java 9+: invokedynamic with StringConcatFactory', 'invokedynamic', 'stringconcatfactory', 'indy'), kp('Compile-time constants folded into one literal', 'constant fold*', 'compile time', 'constant*'), kp('Loops still allocate per iteration', 'loop*', 'per iteration', 'each iteration')],
    'For compile time constants such as "a" + "b", javac folds them into one literal in the constant pool. Up to Java 8, any other a + b + c was compiled into new StringBuilder().append(a).append(b).append(c).toString(). Since Java 9, JEP 280 compiles concatenation to an invokedynamic call bootstrapped by StringConcatFactory, which lets the JVM choose an optimal strategy at runtime, for example computing the exact final length and filling one byte array without intermediate copies. This is faster and can improve with new JVM versions without recompiling. It still optimises only a single expression, so concatenating in a loop allocates on each iteration.',
    'Why did the JDK move string concatenation to invokedynamic?', src(31)),

  Q('jb-032', S, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is the difference between isEmpty() and isBlank()?',
    [kp('isEmpty: length is zero', 'isempty', 'length', 'zero'), kp('isBlank: empty or only whitespace', 'isblank', 'whitespace'), kp('isBlank added in Java 11, Unicode aware', 'java 11', 'unicode'), kp('Both throw NPE on null; use null checks or utilities', 'null', 'nullpointer*', 'stringutils')],
    'isEmpty returns true only when the string length is zero, so " " with spaces is not empty. isBlank, added in Java 11, returns true when the string is empty or contains only whitespace, and it is Unicode aware because it uses Character.isWhitespace, so tabs and newlines also count. For validating user input such as a name field, isBlank is usually what you want. Both are instance methods, so calling them on a null reference throws NullPointerException; you check for null first, or use helpers like StringUtils.isBlank from Apache Commons or Bean Validation annotations such as NotBlank on request objects. strip is the matching Unicode aware trim.',
    'What is the difference between trim() and strip()?', src(32)),

  Q('jb-033', S, 'SL', 'concept', 'How does the Compact Strings feature work in modern Java?',
    [kp('Java 9: String backed by byte[] not char[]', 'byte array', 'byte[]', 'java 9', 'char array'), kp('Coder flag: LATIN1 one byte, UTF16 two bytes', 'coder', 'latin1', 'latin-1', 'utf16', 'utf-16'), kp('Halves memory for Latin-1 text', 'half', 'memory', 'heap'), kp('Transparent to code; can be disabled with flag', 'transparent', 'compactstrings', 'flag')],
    'Before Java 9 a String stored characters in a char array, two bytes per character. Most strings in typical applications are Latin 1, such as ids, JSON keys and English text, so half of that memory was zeros. Compact Strings, JEP 254 in Java 9, changed the backing field to a byte array plus a coder flag. If every character fits in Latin 1 the string uses one byte per character; otherwise it uses UTF 16 with two bytes. This often cuts the heap used by strings almost in half and improves cache behaviour. It is transparent to application code because the String API is unchanged, and it can be disabled with the CompactStrings JVM flag.',
    'How would you measure how much heap your application uses for strings?', src(33)),

  Q('jb-035', J, 'SL', 'scenario', 'How can excessive string creation contribute to memory pressure in a backend application?',
    [kp('Many short-lived strings raise allocation rate', 'allocation rate', 'short lived', 'allocat*'), kp('More frequent young GCs, CPU and latency', 'young gc', 'gc pause*', 'garbage collect*', 'latency'), kp('Sources: logging, JSON, concatenation, toString', 'logging', 'json', 'concatenat*', 'tostring'), kp('Duplicate long-lived strings fill old gen', 'duplicate*', 'old generation', 'old gen', 'cache*'), kp('Fixes: parameterised logs, builders, dedup, profiling', 'parameteri*', 'placeholder*', 'deduplicat*', 'profil*')],
    'Every request in a backend can create thousands of temporary strings: string concatenation in log statements even when the level is disabled, JSON serialisation, toString calls, splitting and formatting. That drives up the allocation rate, so young GCs run more often, burning CPU and adding latency spikes. If many duplicate strings are kept, such as the same status values in a large cache, they survive into the old generation and increase full GC risk. To fix it, use parameterised logging with placeholders, StringBuilder in loops, avoid needless toString, stream JSON, use enums or interning or G1 string deduplication for repeated values, and confirm with an allocation profiler such as JFR or async-profiler.',
    'How would you find which code path allocates the most strings?', src(35)),

  Q('jb-036', CO, 'EM', 'concept', 'Explain the Java Collections Framework hierarchy.',
    [kp('Iterable and Collection at the root', 'iterable', 'collection interface', 'collection'), kp('List, Set, Queue/Deque sub-interfaces', 'list', 'set', 'queue', 'deque'), kp('Map is a separate hierarchy', 'map', 'separate', 'not a collection'), kp('Main implementations', 'arraylist', 'hashset', 'hashmap', 'arraydeque', 'treemap'), kp('Collections and Arrays utility classes', 'collections class', 'utility', 'arrays')],
    'At the root is Iterable, extended by the Collection interface. Collection has three main sub interfaces. List is ordered and allows duplicates, implemented by ArrayList and LinkedList. Set rejects duplicates, implemented by HashSet, LinkedHashSet and TreeSet through SortedSet and NavigableSet. Queue and Deque model FIFO and double ended queues, implemented by ArrayDeque, PriorityQueue and LinkedList. Map is a separate hierarchy, not a Collection, because it stores key value pairs; HashMap, LinkedHashMap and TreeMap are the common implementations, and ConcurrentHashMap lives in java.util.concurrent. The Collections and Arrays utility classes provide sorting, unmodifiable wrappers and factory helpers.',
    'Why does Map not extend Collection?', src(36)),

  Q('jb-037', CO, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the difference between List, Set, and Map?',
    [kp('List: ordered, index access, duplicates allowed', 'list', 'index', 'duplicates allowed', 'ordered'), kp('Set: unique elements', 'set', 'unique', 'no duplicates'), kp('Map: key-value pairs, unique keys', 'map', 'key value', 'unique keys'), kp('Choose by access pattern with examples', 'example*', 'lookup', 'use case*')],
    'A List is an ordered collection that keeps insertion order, allows duplicates and supports access by index, for example the line items of an order in an ArrayList. A Set holds unique elements, so adding a duplicate is ignored; HashSet is unordered, LinkedHashSet keeps insertion order and TreeSet keeps sorted order, which suits a set of user roles. A Map stores key value pairs with unique keys and fast lookup by key, such as a HashMap from product id to product. You choose by access pattern: positional access and duplicates mean a list, uniqueness means a set, and lookup by key means a map.',
    'Which collection would you use to keep the latest 100 unique search terms in order?', src(37)),

  Q('jb-038', CO, 'EM', 'concept', 'How does ArrayList work internally?',
    [kp('Backed by an Object array', 'array', 'elementdata', 'object array'), kp('Default capacity and growth by 1.5x with copy', '1.5', 'grow*', 'capacity', 'arrays.copyof'), kp('O(1) get and amortised add at end', 'constant time', 'o(1)', 'amortised', 'amortized'), kp('Insert/remove in middle shifts elements O(n)', 'shift*', 'o(n)', 'linear'), kp('Not thread-safe, fail-fast iterator', 'not thread safe', 'modcount', 'fail fast')],
    'ArrayList is backed by an Object array called elementData. A new list starts empty and allocates a default capacity of 10 on the first add. When the array is full it grows by about 1.5 times: it creates a bigger array and copies elements with Arrays.copyOf, so add at the end is amortised constant time. get and set by index are O(1) because they are direct array access. Inserting or removing in the middle shifts the following elements with System.arraycopy, which is O(n). It is not thread safe, and it tracks structural changes in modCount so iterators are fail fast. Presizing with the expected capacity avoids repeated copying.',
    'What does trimToSize() do and when is it useful?', src(38)),

  Q('jb-042', CO, 'EM', 'concept', 'Can HashMap contain null keys and null values?',
    [kp('One null key allowed, stored in bucket 0', 'one null key', 'null key', 'bucket 0', 'bucket zero'), kp('Multiple null values allowed', 'null values', 'multiple'), kp('get returning null is ambiguous; use containsKey', 'containskey', 'ambiguous', 'getordefault'), kp('Hashtable and ConcurrentHashMap reject nulls', 'hashtable', 'concurrenthashmap', 'reject*', 'nullpointer*')],
    'Yes. HashMap allows one null key, which it handles specially by treating its hash as zero, so it lives in bucket 0, and putting another null key just replaces that entry. It allows any number of null values. Because of this, get returning null is ambiguous: the key may be missing or mapped to null, so use containsKey or getOrDefault when it matters. TreeMap rejects a null key with natural ordering because it must compare keys. Hashtable and ConcurrentHashMap reject both null keys and null values with a NullPointerException, since in concurrent code you cannot safely tell absent from null between two calls.',
    'Why does ConcurrentHashMap not allow null values?', src(42)),

  Q('jb-057', CO, 'SL', 'scenario', 'Why is HashMap not thread-safe? Explain a real-world problem caused by concurrent modifications.',
    [kp('No synchronisation on put/resize', 'no synchroni*', 'not synchroni*', 'resize', 'unsynchroni*'), kp('Lost updates / race conditions', 'lost update*', 'race condition', 'overwrit*'), kp('Java 7 resize could create a cycle / infinite loop, 100% CPU', 'infinite loop', 'cycle', '100 percent cpu', 'cpu'), kp('Real example: shared cache in a singleton bean', 'singleton', 'shared cache', 'spring bean', 'cache'), kp('Fix with ConcurrentHashMap / immutability', 'concurrenthashmap', 'immutable', 'computeifabsent')],
    'HashMap has no synchronisation. Two threads calling put at the same time can both read the same bucket and one entry overwrites the other, so updates are lost; size can become wrong; and if a resize happens while another thread writes, entries can disappear or a reader can see a half built table. In Java 7 a concurrent resize could even link bucket entries into a cycle, so a later get spun in an infinite loop at 100 percent CPU. A classic real case is a HashMap used as a shared cache inside a singleton Spring bean: under load, lookups hang or return stale data. The fix is ConcurrentHashMap with computeIfAbsent, or immutable maps that are replaced atomically.',
    'Why is Collections.synchronizedMap often worse than ConcurrentHashMap?', src(57)),

  Q('jb-058', CO, 'SL', 'design', 'How would you choose between ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, and ConcurrentHashMap for a high-throughput application?',
    [kp('ArrayList: cache-friendly, fast iteration and index access', 'arraylist', 'cache friendly', 'contiguous', 'iteration'), kp('LinkedList rarely wins: pointer chasing, memory overhead', 'linkedlist', 'pointer chasing', 'node overhead', 'rarely'), kp('HashSet: O(1) membership, needs good hashCode', 'hashset', 'membership', 'contains', 'hashcode'), kp('ConcurrentHashMap: shared mutable state across threads', 'concurrenthashmap', 'concurrent access', 'multiple threads', 'thread safe'), kp('Measure with benchmarks/JMH, size up front', 'jmh', 'benchmark*', 'measure', 'initial capacity')],
    'I start from the access pattern and concurrency. For ordered data that is mostly appended and iterated, ArrayList is the default: its contiguous array is cache friendly and index access is O(1). LinkedList rarely wins even for inserts, because pointer chasing and per node overhead hurt CPU caches; ArrayDeque is better for queues. For fast membership checks and de duplication I use HashSet, which gives O(1) contains if hashCode is well distributed. When many threads read and update shared state, such as counters or a cache, I use ConcurrentHashMap with merge or computeIfAbsent rather than locking a HashMap. I size collections with an initial capacity and confirm choices with JMH benchmarks and profiling.',
    'When would you use an immutable copy instead of a concurrent collection?', src(58)),

  Q('jb-059', J, 'SL', 'scenario', 'How would you optimize a collection holding millions of objects?',
    [kp('Measure footprint first (heap dump, JOL)', 'heap dump', 'jol', 'measure', 'footprint'), kp('Presize to avoid resizing', 'presize', 'initial capacity', 'resiz*'), kp('Primitive collections / arrays instead of boxing', 'primitive collection*', 'boxing', 'fastutil', 'eclipse collections', 'int array'), kp('Smaller objects: fewer fields, compact representation, dedup', 'fewer fields', 'compact', 'deduplicat*', 'object header*'), kp('Do not keep everything in memory: stream, page, offload', 'stream*', 'pagin*', 'off heap', 'database', 'cache')],
    'First I measure: a heap dump in Eclipse MAT or Java Object Layout shows the real footprint per entry, which is often several times the raw data because of object headers, references and boxing. Then I presize the collection with an initial capacity to avoid repeated resizing and copying. If the values are numbers I replace boxed collections with primitive collections such as fastutil or Eclipse Collections, or plain int arrays. I shrink the element objects by removing fields, using compact types and deduplicating repeated strings. Finally I question whether all of it must live in memory at once: stream or page the data from the database, or move it into an off heap cache like Redis.',
    'How much memory does a HashMap entry really cost?', src(59)),

  Q('jb-061', M, 'EM', 'concept', 'What are the major features introduced in Java 8?',
    [kp('Lambda expressions and functional interfaces', 'lambda*', 'functional interface*'), kp('Stream API', 'stream*'), kp('Optional', 'optional'), kp('Default and static methods in interfaces', 'default method*', 'static method*'), kp('java.time Date-Time API, CompletableFuture, method references', 'java.time', 'date time', 'localdate', 'completablefuture', 'method reference*')],
    'Java 8 was the biggest change to the language in years. It added lambda expressions and functional interfaces such as Function, Predicate and Supplier, plus method references like String::length. The Stream API brought declarative filter, map and collect pipelines, including parallel streams. Optional made possibly absent return values explicit. Interfaces gained default and static methods, which let the JDK evolve interfaces such as Collection without breaking implementations. The new java.time Date Time API, with LocalDate, Instant and ZonedDateTime, replaced the mutable Date and Calendar classes. It also introduced CompletableFuture for asynchronous composition, and Metaspace replaced PermGen.',
    'Why were default methods needed to add the Stream API?', src(61)),

  Q('jb-062', M, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is a lambda expression?',
    [kp('Anonymous function: parameters -> body', 'anonymous function', 'arrow', 'parameters'), kp('Implements a functional interface', 'functional interface', 'single abstract method'), kp('Captures effectively final variables', 'effectively final', 'captur*'), kp('Concise vs anonymous class, uses invokedynamic', 'anonymous class', 'concise', 'invokedynamic', 'boilerplate')],
    'A lambda expression is an anonymous function: a block of behaviour with parameters, an arrow and a body, such as (a, b) -> a + b or order -> order.total() > 100. Its type is always a functional interface, an interface with a single abstract method, so a lambda can be passed where a Comparator, Runnable or Predicate is expected. It is much more concise than an anonymous inner class, removing boilerplate. Lambdas can capture local variables only if they are final or effectively final. Under the hood the compiler uses invokedynamic and LambdaMetafactory, so no separate class file is generated per lambda, and this is not bound to a new object as in anonymous classes.',
    'How does this behave differently inside a lambda versus an anonymous class?', src(62)),

  Q('jb-064', M, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the purpose of the @FunctionalInterface annotation?',
    [kp('Marks an interface intended to have one abstract method', 'one abstract method', 'single abstract method', 'functional interface'), kp('Compiler error if the contract is broken', 'compile* error', 'compiler', 'enforce*'), kp('Optional: any SAM interface works with lambdas', 'optional', 'not required', 'without the annotation'), kp('Default/static methods and Object methods allowed', 'default method*', 'static method*', 'object methods')],
    'FunctionalInterface documents that an interface is meant to have exactly one abstract method, so it can be the target of a lambda or method reference. Its real value is that the compiler enforces it: if someone later adds a second abstract method, compilation fails with an error instead of silently breaking every lambda that used the interface. The annotation is optional; any interface with a single abstract method works with lambdas without the annotation. The interface may still declare default methods and static methods, and abstract methods that override public methods of Object, such as equals, do not count. Examples are Runnable, Comparator and Function.',
    'Why does Comparator still count as a functional interface although it declares equals?', src(64)),

  Q('jb-065', M, 'EM', 'concept', 'What is the Stream API?',
    [kp('Declarative pipeline over a data source', 'pipeline', 'declarative', 'source'), kp('Intermediate (filter, map) and terminal (collect) operations', 'intermediate', 'terminal', 'filter', 'collect'), kp('Lazy and does not store or modify the source', 'lazy', 'does not store', 'not modify', 'single use'), kp('Parallel streams possible', 'parallel'), kp('Example', 'example', 'orders', 'employees')],
    'The Stream API, added in Java 8, lets you process data declaratively as a pipeline instead of writing explicit loops. A stream comes from a source such as a collection, an array or Files.lines, then has zero or more intermediate operations such as filter, map and sorted, and ends with one terminal operation such as collect, count or forEach. Streams do not store data and do not modify the source; they are lazy, so nothing runs until the terminal operation, and each stream can be consumed only once. For example, orders.stream().filter(Order::isPaid).map(Order::total).reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add). A stream can also run as a parallel stream on the ForkJoinPool.',
    'Why can a stream only be consumed once?', src(65)),

  Q('jb-067', M, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is the difference between filter(), map(), and reduce()?',
    [kp('filter keeps elements matching a Predicate', 'filter', 'predicate', 'keeps'), kp('map transforms each element with a Function', 'map', 'transform*', 'function'), kp('reduce combines elements into one result', 'reduce', 'combin*', 'single result', 'accumulat*'), kp('filter/map intermediate, reduce terminal', 'intermediate', 'terminal'), kp('Example', 'example', 'sum', 'total')],
    'filter takes a Predicate and keeps only the elements that match, so the stream may get shorter but the type stays the same. map takes a Function and transforms each element into a new value, possibly of a different type, one output per input. Both are lazy intermediate operations. reduce is a terminal operation that combines all elements into a single result using an identity and an associative accumulator. For example, to total paid orders: orders.stream().filter(Order::isPaid).map(Order::amount).reduce(0L, Long::sum). In practice specialised reductions such as sum, count, or collect with Collectors are often clearer than a hand written reduce.',
    'Why must the accumulator in reduce be associative?', src(67)),

  Q('jb-069', M, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is lazy evaluation in Java Streams?',
    [kp('Intermediate operations do nothing until a terminal operation', 'terminal operation', 'nothing runs', 'until'), kp('Elements flow one at a time through the whole pipeline', 'one at a time', 'vertical*', 'each element'), kp('Short-circuiting: findFirst, limit, anyMatch', 'short circuit*', 'findfirst', 'limit', 'anymatch'), kp('Enables infinite streams and avoids extra work', 'infinite', 'iterate', 'avoid*', 'efficien*')],
    'Lazy evaluation means intermediate operations such as filter and map only describe the pipeline; nothing runs until a terminal operation such as collect or findFirst is called. When it runs, elements flow one at a time vertically through the whole pipeline, rather than filtering the entire list and then mapping the entire list. Combined with short circuiting operations such as findFirst, anyMatch and limit, the stream stops as soon as it has the answer, so it may touch only a few elements. That avoids extra work and intermediate collections, and it makes infinite streams like Stream.iterate possible. A peek with a println shows this order clearly.',
    'What does Stream.of(1,2,3).map(x -> {print(x); return x;}) print without a terminal operation?', src(69)),

  Q('jb-070', M, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is the difference between findFirst() and findAny()?',
    [kp('findFirst returns the first element in encounter order', 'findfirst', 'encounter order', 'first element'), kp('findAny may return any element', 'findany', 'any element'), kp('Same in sequential streams, differs in parallel', 'parallel', 'sequential'), kp('findAny is cheaper in parallel; both return Optional', 'cheaper', 'faster', 'optional', 'short circuit*')],
    'Both are short circuiting terminal operations that return an Optional. findFirst returns the first element in the encounter order of the stream, so the result is deterministic. findAny is allowed to return any element that matches. In a sequential stream they normally return the same element. The difference matters with a parallel stream: findFirst has to coordinate between threads to respect encounter order, which costs performance, while findAny can return whichever element a worker finds first, so it is cheaper but nondeterministic. Use findFirst when order matters and findAny when you only need some match.',
    'What happens if the element found by findFirst is null?', src(70)),

  Q('jb-074', M, 'MS', 'concept', 'What is the difference between orElse() and orElseGet()?',
    [kp('orElse argument is always evaluated', 'orelse', 'always evaluated', 'eager*'), kp('orElseGet takes a Supplier evaluated only when empty', 'orelseget', 'supplier', 'only when empty', 'lazy', 'lazily'), kp('Matters for expensive or side-effecting defaults', 'expensive', 'side effect*', 'database', 'remote call'), kp('orElseThrow for missing values', 'orelsethrow', 'throw*')],
    'orElse takes a value, so its argument is always evaluated eagerly, even when the Optional contains a value. orElseGet takes a Supplier, and the supplier is only called when the Optional is empty, so evaluation is lazy. With a constant default like orElse("unknown") there is no difference. It matters when the default is expensive or has side effects, for example orElse(createDefaultUser()) would call the method, perhaps insert a row in the database or make a remote call, on every invocation even when a user was found. In that case use orElseGet(this::createDefaultUser). When absence is an error, orElseThrow with a specific exception is clearer.',
    'Why is Optional.get() discouraged?', src(74)),

  Q('jb-078', M, 'SL', 'concept', 'What is the difference between stateless and stateful stream operations?',
    [kp('Stateless: each element processed independently (filter, map)', 'stateless', 'independent*', 'filter', 'map'), kp('Stateful: need other elements (sorted, distinct, limit)', 'stateful', 'sorted', 'distinct'), kp('Stateful ops may buffer the whole stream, memory cost', 'buffer*', 'entire stream', 'memory'), kp('Hurt parallel performance and infinite streams', 'parallel', 'infinite')],
    'A stateless operation such as filter, map or flatMap processes each element independently, without knowing anything about other elements, so it can stream through data element by element and parallelise easily. A stateful operation such as sorted, distinct, limit or skip needs information about other elements. sorted must buffer the entire stream before emitting anything, and distinct must remember every element seen so far, which costs memory. Stateful operations are barriers in parallel streams, need coordination and often reduce the speed up; sorted on an infinite stream never finishes. Lambdas themselves should also be stateless, without mutating shared variables, to stay correct in parallel.',
    'Why is limit() expensive on an ordered parallel stream?', src(78)),

  Q('jb-079', M, 'SL', 'scenario', 'How would you process a large collection without creating unnecessary intermediate objects?',
    [kp('Single lazy stream pipeline rather than intermediate lists', 'single pipeline', 'lazy', 'intermediate list*', 'intermediate collection*'), kp('Primitive streams avoid boxing', 'intstream', 'longstream', 'primitive stream*', 'boxing'), kp('Collect directly into the needed structure', 'collect', 'collectors', 'groupingby'), kp('Stream from the source in batches (files, DB cursors)', 'batch*', 'files.lines', 'cursor', 'fetch size'), kp('Plain loops when hot, profile allocations', 'loop', 'profil*', 'allocation*')],
    'I keep the work in one lazy pipeline instead of building intermediate lists at each step, so filter, map and the final collect process each element once. For numbers I use primitive streams like IntStream, LongStream or mapToLong to avoid boxing every value. I collect directly into the structure I need, for example groupingBy with a downstream counting or summing collector, instead of collecting and then transforming again. If the data comes from a file or database I stream it from the source in batches with Files.lines, a JDBC cursor with a fetch size, or pagination rather than loading everything. On very hot paths a plain loop is fine, and I confirm with an allocation profiler.',
    'How would you sum the amount per customer for a million orders efficiently?', src(79)),

  Q('jb-083', E, 'EM', 'concept', 'Explain try, catch, finally, throw, and throws.',
    [kp('try wraps code that may throw', 'try block', 'try'), kp('catch handles specific exceptions', 'catch'), kp('finally always runs for cleanup', 'finally', 'cleanup', 'always'), kp('throw raises an exception object', 'throw '), kp('throws declares checked exceptions in the signature', 'throws', 'signature', 'checked')],
    'try wraps code that might throw an exception. One or more catch blocks follow, each handling a specific exception type, ordered from most specific to most general, or combined with multi catch. finally always runs after try and catch, whether an exception was thrown or not, so it is used for cleanup such as releasing locks, although try with resources is preferred for closing resources. throw is a statement that raises an exception object, for example throw new IllegalArgumentException("amount must be positive"). throws is part of a method signature and declares the checked exceptions the method may propagate to its caller, such as IOException.',
    'Why should catch blocks go from specific to general?', src(83)),

  Q('jb-084', E, 'MS', 'concept', 'Can finally execute without a catch block?',
    [kp('Yes: try-finally is valid', 'try finally', 'yes'), kp('finally runs, then the exception propagates', 'propagat*', 'still thrown', 'caller'), kp('Used for cleanup like unlocking', 'unlock', 'cleanup', 'release'), kp('try-with-resources is the modern alternative for closing', 'try with resources', 'autocloseable')],
    'Yes. A try block must be followed by at least one catch or a finally, so try with only finally is valid. If the try block throws, the finally block runs first and then the exception propagates to the caller, because nothing caught it. This is the standard pattern for cleanup that must happen regardless of outcome but where the method should not handle the error itself, for example lock.lock(); try { work } finally { lock.unlock(); }. For resources that implement AutoCloseable, such as streams and JDBC connections, try with resources is the modern alternative, since it closes them automatically and keeps suppressed exceptions.',
    'Why must lock.lock() be called before the try block and not inside it?', src(84)),

  Q('jb-085', E, 'MS', 'concept', 'Can finally ever fail to execute?',
    [kp('System.exit / Runtime.halt', 'system.exit', 'halt', 'exit'), kp('JVM crash or killed process (kill -9, OOM killer)', 'crash', 'kill -9', 'sigkill', 'killed'), kp('Infinite loop or deadlock in try', 'infinite loop', 'deadlock', 'never finishes'), kp('Daemon thread when JVM exits; power loss', 'daemon thread', 'power'), kp('Otherwise runs even with return', 'return', 'otherwise')],
    'Normally finally runs even if the try block executes a return, a break or throws. It can fail to execute in a few cases. If the code calls System.exit or Runtime.halt inside try or catch, the JVM shuts down without running finally. If the JVM crashes, or the process is killed with kill -9 or by the Kubernetes OOM killer, nothing more runs. If the try block never finishes because of an infinite loop or a deadlock, finally is never reached. A daemon thread is abandoned when the JVM exits, so its finally may not run, and of course power loss or hardware failure stops everything. So finally is not a guarantee for critical cross process state.',
    'What happens if both try and finally contain a return statement?', src(85)),

  Q('jb-086', E, 'MS', 'concept', 'What happens when an exception is thrown inside a finally block?',
    [kp('It replaces/masks the original exception', 'mask*', 'replace*', 'lost', 'original exception'), kp('The original from try is lost', 'lost', 'swallow*'), kp('Same for return in finally', 'return in finally', 'return'), kp('Avoid: catch inside finally or use try-with-resources (suppressed)', 'suppressed', 'try with resources', 'catch inside')],
    'The exception thrown from finally replaces the one that was propagating from the try or catch block, so the original exception is lost and the caller sees only the new one. That makes debugging very hard, because the root cause disappears and you only see, for example, an error from close. A return statement in finally similarly swallows any exception. To avoid this, keep finally blocks simple and wrap risky cleanup in its own try and catch inside finally, logging the cleanup failure. Better, use try with resources: if close fails after the body has thrown, the close exception is attached to the original as a suppressed exception instead of replacing it.',
    'How do you read suppressed exceptions from a caught exception?', src(86)),

  Q('jb-087', E, 'MS', 'concept', 'How do you create custom exceptions?',
    [kp('Extend Exception (checked) or RuntimeException (unchecked)', 'extend*', 'runtimeexception', 'exception class'), kp('Constructors with message and cause', 'message', 'cause', 'constructor*'), kp('Meaningful name and extra context fields', 'meaningful', 'error code', 'context', 'field*'), kp('Map to HTTP responses / handle centrally', 'controlleradvice', 'http', 'status', 'handler')],
    'You create a class that extends Exception for a checked exception or RuntimeException for an unchecked one; in Spring services most business exceptions are unchecked so they do not pollute every signature. Give it a meaningful name such as InsufficientBalanceException, and constructors that accept a message and a cause, calling super(message, cause) so the original stack trace is preserved when wrapping. Add context fields where useful, such as an error code or the account id, but never sensitive data. In a REST application a ControllerAdvice handler maps each custom exception to the right HTTP status and a consistent error response.',
    'When would you choose a checked custom exception over an unchecked one?', src(87)),

  Q('jb-091', E, 'SL', 'design', 'How do you distinguish between business exceptions, technical exceptions, and infrastructure failures?',
    [kp('Business: expected rule violations, 4xx, no retry', 'business exception*', 'business rule*', '4xx', 'validation'), kp('Technical: bugs/unexpected states, 500, alert', 'technical exception*', 'bug*', '500', 'unexpected'), kp('Infrastructure: DB/network/downstream failures, often transient, retry', 'infrastructure', 'timeout*', 'transient', 'retry'), kp('Separate exception hierarchies and handlers', 'hierarch*', 'base class', 'handler*'), kp('Different logging levels, metrics and alerting', 'log level*', 'warn', 'error level', 'alert*', 'metric*')],
    'Business exceptions are expected outcomes of business rules, such as insufficient balance or an order already shipped. They are not bugs, map to 4xx responses with a clear error code, are logged at info or warn level and are never retried. Technical exceptions are unexpected states caused by bugs, like a NullPointerException or an illegal state; they map to 500, are logged at error level with the stack trace and should alert someone. Infrastructure failures come from the environment: database or network timeouts, a downstream service unavailable, broker errors. They are often transient, so retries with backoff, circuit breakers and 503 responses fit. I model them as separate exception hierarchies with dedicated handlers, metrics and alerting.',
    'How would your retry logic avoid retrying business exceptions?', src(91)),

  Q('jb-092', T, 'SL', 'scenario', 'How would you troubleshoot an application that frequently logs NullPointerException errors?',
    [kp('Group by stack trace and frequency, find the top source', 'stack trace*', 'group*', 'frequen*', 'top'), kp('Helpful NPE messages show which variable', 'helpful', 'jep 358', 'which variable', 'message*'), kp('Trace back to data: missing fields, external responses, config', 'missing field*', 'external', 'input', 'data'), kp('Reproduce with a test, then fix root cause', 'reproduc*', 'unit test', 'root cause'), kp('Prevent: validation, Optional, non-null contracts, static analysis', 'validat*', 'optional', 'nonnull', 'static analysis', 'nullaway')],
    'First I aggregate the logs, for example in Kibana or Grafana Loki, and group the errors by stack trace and frequency to find the few top sources rather than chasing every one. On Java 14 and later, helpful NullPointerException messages tell exactly which variable or method result was null, which narrows it quickly. Then I trace the null back to its origin: a missing field in a request, an unexpected external API response, an empty database column, a missing config value or an uninitialised bean. I reproduce it with a unit test and fix the root cause rather than adding a null check everywhere. To prevent it I validate input at the boundary, use Optional for absent results, enforce non null contracts and run static analysis like NullAway.',
    'Why is catching NullPointerException usually the wrong fix?', src(92)),
];
